{"type": "assistant", "uuid": "82fcee43-e3a7-4245-abe6-ebe66e9abc56", "parentUuid": "c7a8c8e7-1976-49e5-bfc5-8eda2cda1e99", "sessionId": "00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001", "isSidechain": false, "timestamp": "2026-11-22T01:43:25.605Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "id": "msg_01", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "# Mutex vs. Semaphore\n\nA mutex and a semaphore are both synchronization primitives that coordinate access to shared resources in concurrent programs. They are built for different jobs, and using one where the other belongs is a common source of bugs.\n\n## The core idea\n\nA **mutex** (short for \"mutual exclusion\") is a lock. Only one thread can hold it at a time. A thread acquires the mutex, enters a critical section, does its work and releases the mutex. Any other thread that tries to acquire it in the meantime blocks until it is released. Think of it as the single key to a bathroom: whoever has the key goes in, and everyone else waits.\n\nA **semaphore** is a counter with two atomic operations, usually called `wait` (or `P`, or `acquire`) and `signal` (or `V`, or `release`). `wait` decrements the counter and blocks if the result would go below zero. `signal` increments the counter and wakes a waiting thread if there is one. A semaphore initialized to *N* lets up to *N* threads proceed at once. Think of a parking lot with *N* spaces and a sign at the entrance showing how many are free.\n\n## Ownership\n\nThe most important difference is **ownership**. A mutex has an owner: only the thread that locked it may unlock it. Many implementations enforce this, and the rule makes several features possible:\n\n- **Priority inheritance**: if a low-priority thread holds a mutex that a high-priority thread needs, the system can temporarily boost the holder's priority to prevent priority inversion.\n- **Recursive locking**: a reentrant mutex can be locked several times by the thread that owns it.\n- **Error detection**: the system can flag an unlock by the wrong thread, or a thread that exits while still holding a lock.\n\nA semaphore has no owner. Any thread can signal it, including one that never waited on it. That sounds like a weakness, but it is exactly what makes semaphores useful for **signaling between threads**. A producer can signal a semaphore each time it adds an item to a queue, and a consumer waits on it before taking one off. Here the semaphore counts events or resources. It is not protecting a critical section.\n\n## Binary semaphores are not mutexes\n\nA semaphore initialized to 1 (a \"binary semaphore\") looks like a mutex, but it lacks ownership semantics. Because any thread can release it, it offers no protection against accidental unlocks and no priority inheritance. When the goal is protecting shared data, a real mutex is the safer choice.\n\n## Rule of thumb\n\n- Use a **mutex** to protect shared state so that one thread at a time touches it.\n- Use a **semaphore** to limit concurrency to *N* (such as a connection pool) or to signal that something happened (such as producer/consumer coordination).\n\nIn short, a mutex is about *exclusive ownership* and a semaphore is about *counting*."}]}}
